---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import MedicalDisclaimer from '../components/MedicalDisclaimer.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="GFR Formulas & Methodology: CKD-EPI, MDRD, Cockcroft-Gault"
	description="The formulas behind this GFR calculator: CKD-EPI, MDRD, Cockcroft-Gault, cystatin C, and Schwartz, with constants, sources, and how we test unit conversion."
>
	<article class="container-page py-14 sm:py-20">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-2xl">
			<p class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-mute">Methodology</p>
			<h1 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">
				How this calculator works
			</h1>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				This page documents the formulas, unit-conversion approach, and design decisions behind
				this calculator, and the research that motivated them.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">
				Why accuracy needed to be the starting point
			</h2>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				In 2020, Seiberth, Terstegen, Strobach, and Czock published
				<a
					href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7481157/"
					target="_blank"
					rel="noopener noreferrer"
					class="text-link hover:text-link-deep"
				>
					&ldquo;Accuracy of freely available online GFR calculators using the CKD-EPI equation&rdquo;
				</a>
				in the <em>European Journal of Clinical Pharmacology</em>. The authors tested 47 freely
				available online CKD-EPI calculators against five hand-verified reference cases and found:
			</p>
			<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				<li>Only 36 of 47 calculators (76.6%) produced accurate results for every reference case.</li>
				<li>
					8 of 47 (17.0%) were considered <strong>faulty</strong> — errors that could lead to
					inappropriate clinical decisions.
				</li>
				<li>
					The errors broke down as: 4 calculators mishandling ethnicity/race, 2 miscalculating the
					conversion from indexed eGFR (mL/min/1.73m&sup2;) to non-indexed eGFR (mL/min), 1
					converting serum creatinine units (mg/dL &harr; &micro;mol/L) incorrectly, and 1
					mislabeling the eGFR unit displayed to the user.
				</li>
				<li>Errors ranged from 59% to 147% of the correct value — large enough to change a clinical decision.</li>
				<li>Only 7 of 47 (14.9%) calculators even offered eGFR unit conversion in the first place.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				That paper is the direct reason this site centralizes creatinine unit conversion into a
				single, tested function (<code class="rounded-sm bg-hairline-soft px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm">convertCreatinine()</code>),
				does not implement a race-based eGFR adjustment for any formula, states clearly which units
				each formula returns (mL/min/1.73m&sup2; for the three eGFR equations vs. mL/min for
				Cockcroft-Gault), and shows every intermediate calculation step so a result can be checked
				by hand.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Formulas used</h2>

			<div class="mt-6 space-y-8">
				<div>
					<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">CKD-EPI 2021 (race-free)</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
						The current standard recommended by the National Kidney Foundation (NKF) and American
						Society of Nephrology (ASN) for eGFR reporting in the United States. Removed the race
						coefficient present in the 2009 equation after the NKF/ASN Task Force found it
						systematically overestimated kidney function in Black patients.
					</p>
					<pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-hairline bg-hairline-soft p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink">eGFR = 142 &times; min(Scr/&kappa;, 1)^&alpha; &times; max(Scr/&kappa;, 1)^&minus;1.200 &times; 0.9938^Age &times; 1.012 [if female]
&kappa; = 0.7 (female) / 0.9 (male)
&alpha; = -0.241 (female) / -0.302 (male)</pre>
					<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-faint">Source: Inker LA, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385:1737-1749.</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">CKD-EPI 2009 (classic)</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
						The predecessor to the 2021 equation, still referenced by some older lab reports. The
						original version included a &times;1.159 coefficient for patients identified as Black.
						This site omits that coefficient consistently with the race-free approach used across
						every formula here — see the note below each result on the calculator.
					</p>
					<pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-hairline bg-hairline-soft p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink">eGFR = 141 &times; min(Scr/&kappa;, 1)^&alpha; &times; max(Scr/&kappa;, 1)^&minus;1.209 &times; 0.993^Age &times; 1.018 [if female]
&kappa; = 0.7 (female) / 0.9 (male)
&alpha; = -0.329 (female) / -0.411 (male)</pre>
					<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-faint">Source: Levey AS, et al. Ann Intern Med. 2009;150:604-612.</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">MDRD (4-variable)</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
						An older equation, still cited on some lab reports, that tends to underestimate GFR
						above roughly 60 mL/min/1.73m&sup2;. As with CKD-EPI 2009, this site omits the
						original &times;1.212 Black race coefficient.
					</p>
					<pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-hairline bg-hairline-soft p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink">eGFR = 175 &times; Scr^&minus;1.154 &times; Age^&minus;0.203 &times; 0.742 [if female]</pre>
					<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-faint">Source: Levey AS, et al. Ann Intern Med. 2006;145:247-254.</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">Cockcroft-Gault</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
						Estimates creatinine clearance in mL/min — not normalized to body surface area, and
						not directly comparable to the eGFR values above. Requires body weight. Commonly used
						for drug-dosing calculations rather than general kidney function screening.
					</p>
					<pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-hairline bg-hairline-soft p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink">CrCl (mL/min) = [(140 - Age) &times; Weight(kg)] / (72 &times; Scr) &times; 0.85 [if female]</pre>
					<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-faint">Source: Cockcroft DW, Gault MH. Nephron. 1976;16:31-41.</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">CKD-EPI cystatin C (2012) and creatinine-cystatin C (2021)</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
						Used on the <a href="/cystatin-c-gfr-calculator" class="text-link hover:text-link-deep">cystatin C GFR calculator</a>.
						Neither equation uses race. KDIGO suggests them when creatinine is likely to be less accurate.
					</p>
					<pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-hairline bg-hairline-soft p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink">eGFRcys = 133 &times; min(Scys/0.8, 1)^&minus;0.499 &times; max(Scys/0.8, 1)^&minus;1.328 &times; 0.996^Age &times; 0.932 [if female]

eGFRcr-cys = 135 &times; min(Scr/&kappa;, 1)^&alpha; &times; max(Scr/&kappa;, 1)^&minus;0.544 &times; min(Scys/0.8, 1)^&minus;0.323
             &times; max(Scys/0.8, 1)^&minus;0.778 &times; 0.9961^Age &times; 0.963 [if female]
&kappa; = 0.7 (female) / 0.9 (male)
&alpha; = -0.219 (female) / -0.144 (male)</pre>
					<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-faint">Sources: Inker LA, et al. N Engl J Med. 2012;367:20-29. Inker LA, et al. N Engl J Med. 2021;385:1737-1749.</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink">Bedside Schwartz (children)</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
						Used on the <a href="/pediatric-gfr-calculator" class="text-link hover:text-link-deep">pediatric GFR calculator</a>.
						Developed in children aged 1 to 16 with CKD, using IDMS-traceable creatinine.
					</p>
					<pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-hairline bg-hairline-soft p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink">eGFR = 0.413 &times; Height (cm) / Scr (mg/dL)</pre>
					<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-faint">Source: Schwartz GJ, et al. J Am Soc Nephrol. 2009;20:629-637.</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Unit conversion</h2>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				Serum creatinine can be reported in mg/dL (common in the US) or &micro;mol/L (common
				elsewhere). This site uses the standard conversion factor:
			</p>
			<pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-hairline bg-hairline-soft p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink">1 mg/dL &asymp; 88.4 &micro;mol/L</pre>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				All formulas above are defined in terms of mg/dL, so any &micro;mol/L input is converted to
				mg/dL first, in a single shared function, before any formula runs. This conversion step is
				shown explicitly in every result&rsquo;s step-by-step working.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">CKD stage classification</h2>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				Results are classified into KDIGO CKD stages based on eGFR in mL/min/1.73m&sup2;:
			</p>
			<div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-md border border-hairline">
				<table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
					<thead>
						<tr class="border-b border-hairline bg-hairline-soft">
							<th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">Stage</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">eGFR (mL/min/1.73m&sup2;)</th>
							<th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">Description</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline">
						<tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">G1</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">&ge; 90</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Normal or high kidney function</td></tr>
						<tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">G2</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">60&ndash;89</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Mildly decreased kidney function</td></tr>
						<tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">G3a</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">45&ndash;59</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Mildly to moderately decreased</td></tr>
						<tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">G3b</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">30&ndash;44</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Moderately to severely decreased</td></tr>
						<tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">G4</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">15&ndash;29</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Severely decreased kidney function</td></tr>
						<tr><td class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium text-ink">G5</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">&lt; 15</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-body">Kidney failure</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				A CKD diagnosis requires a reduced eGFR (or other markers of kidney damage, such as
				albuminuria) to persist for three months or more — a single calculation here is not
				sufficient to diagnose CKD on its own.
			</p>

			<h2 class="mt-12 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Testing</h2>
			<p class="mt-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-body">
				The calculation engine behind this site has an automated test suite covering unit
				conversion in both directions, CKD stage boundary conditions, and each formula against
				independently hand-verified reference values, so that regressions are caught before they
				reach a result on this page.
			</p>

			<div class="mt-12">
				<MedicalDisclaimer />
			</div>
		</div>
	</article>
</Layout>
